1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,777
Shopping agents are great at finding a cheap price.

2
00:00:03,527 --> 00:00:07,103
But a cheap price is useless if it is for the wrong product.

3
00:00:07,603 --> 00:00:10,959
SKUdesk puts one gate in front of every number:

4
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:15,717
is this listing provably the same item? If nothing is, the answer is no.

5
00:00:16,217 --> 00:00:22,893
A live check on Google Cloud. SKUdesk searches Google Shopping, and Gemini judges every listing.

6
00:00:22,643 --> 00:00:28,048
Green is the identical product, red is something else, amber is not provable.

7
00:00:28,548 --> 00:00:32,832
It answers in plain terms: what to buy, what a resale would keep,

8
00:00:32,582 --> 00:00:35,315
and whether any gap survives every cost.

9
00:00:35,815 --> 00:00:39,265
The price ladder shows every offer that matched,

10
00:00:39,015 --> 00:00:44,399
and each carries the brand and set number Gemini quoted, which code verifies.

11
00:00:44,899 --> 00:00:48,858
Nothing is bought without a person. The agent files a proposal,

12
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:52,861
and only the person who ran the check, or the owner, can approve it.

13
00:00:53,361 --> 00:00:58,090
LEGO Titanic: seven of thirty listings pass. Two more match the set

14
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:03,036
but sit at eleven and nineteen dollars, so they are set aside as bad data.

15
00:01:02,786 --> 00:01:07,983
The cheapest real offer loses money on resale. The answer is no arbitrage.

16
00:01:08,483 --> 00:01:14,334
Every check can be replayed line by line: the search, each verdict,

17
00:01:14,084 --> 00:01:18,849
the evidence code verified, and the proposal it filed.

18
00:01:19,349 --> 00:01:23,315
The offer market tracks each product's ladder of identical offers.

19
00:01:23,065 --> 00:01:26,749
A daily Cloud Scheduler scan keeps the price history growing.

20
00:01:27,249 --> 00:01:32,140
Gemini is not simply trusted. It must quote the brand and set number,

21
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:37,789
and code checks the quotes are really there. A claim it cannot back up never passes.

22
00:01:38,289 --> 00:01:42,246
Across forty-three hand-labelled pairs against the real model,

23
00:01:41,996 --> 00:01:46,252
zero wrong matches passed. The limits of that test are on the page.

24
00:01:46,752 --> 00:01:51,858
The owner holds the controls: a per-buy limit, a margin floor,

25
00:01:51,608 --> 00:01:58,436
and a kill switch that stops scans, proposals and approvals. Every change is logged.

26
00:01:58,936 --> 00:02:02,745
A what-if simulator runs the same economics as a real check,

27
00:02:02,495 --> 00:02:05,889
so you can test any price against the current limits.

28
00:02:06,389 --> 00:02:11,226
It runs on Cloud Run with Firestore, Cloud Scheduler and Secret Manager,

29
00:02:10,976 --> 00:02:15,559
under a strict content policy, with rate limits and a search budget.

30
00:02:16,059 --> 00:02:20,562
SKUdesk. If nothing is provably the same, it says no.

